<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- THE CONVENTIONAL ENTRY POINT: /sitemap.xml (#11615), and ONE SAME-HOST INDEX (#11766).
     Nothing served this path until #11615, so every crawler, SEO tool and Search
     Console "add sitemap" box that reaches for the conventional URL got a 404 —
     while robots.txt advertised four real sitemaps under names nobody probes.

     ⛔ EVERY <loc> BELOW IS ON THE INDEX'S OWN HOST, `premiselabs.co`. That is the
     Sitemaps protocol's own rule for index files (sitemaps.org, "Using Sitemap index
     files": an index "can only specify Sitemaps that are found on the same site as the
     Sitemap index file … not on … http://yourhost.yoursite.com" — a SUBDOMAIN is the
     spec's own counterexample). It was violated on purpose while the product and blog
     lived on `tortoise.premiselabs.co`; epic #11762 moved every one of those pages onto
     this host, so the deviation is GONE and its `OVERRIDES:` line is deleted rather
     than reworded (#11623 was the cross-host child that made the violation real).

     `app.premiselabs.co/sitemap.xml` is deliberately NOT a child. It was never a
     marketing sitemap: it belongs to the SEPARATE `tortoise-dashboard` Pages project,
     sits behind auth and has its own Search Console property. It is advertised by that
     project's own `apps/dashboard/public/robots.txt`, so dropping it here removes the
     cross-host entry without claiming anything about the app's own property.

     The three children must stay REACHABLE from robots.txt — that file advertises this
     index, and `test_the_sitemap_index_and_robots_advertise_the_same_set` walks the
     relation (robots → index → children) so a child cannot become undiscoverable by
     being dropped here. blog/sitemap.xml is dynamic (published posts only); the other
     two are files in this tree. -->
<sitemapindex xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  <sitemap>
    <loc>https://premiselabs.co/sitemap-company.xml</loc>
  </sitemap>
  <sitemap>
    <loc>https://premiselabs.co/sitemap-product.xml</loc>
  </sitemap>
  <sitemap>
    <loc>https://premiselabs.co/blog/sitemap.xml</loc>
  </sitemap>
</sitemapindex>
